From: Banana <bananalinux@proton.me>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 16:10:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] output: turn the output with the connector's panel orientation

A panel mounted turned inside the shell advertises the turn on its DRM
connector as the "panel orientation" property: on the Retroid Pocket Classic the
device tree's rotation = <90> for its 1080x1240 screen in a landscape body, on a
desktop a monitor whose EDID says so. wlroots reads the property but leaves the
choice to the compositor -- wlr_drm_connector_get_panel_orientation() is public
API for exactly this, and sway calls it when it configures an output. Cage never
did, so on such a panel everything it composes came out sideways: the
application, and the cursor on top of it.

Set it where the new output's state is built, from the preferred mode and the
transform the connector advertises.

Upstream-Status: Pending
---
--- a/output.c
+++ b/output.c
@@ -18,6 +18,9 @@
 #include <wlr/backend.h>
 #include <wlr/backend/wayland.h>
 #include <wlr/config.h>
+#if WLR_HAS_DRM_BACKEND
+#include <wlr/backend/drm.h>
+#endif
 #if WLR_HAS_X11_BACKEND
 #include <wlr/backend/x11.h>
 #endif
@@ -301,6 +304,22 @@
 			wlr_output->scale);
 	}
 
+#if WLR_HAS_DRM_BACKEND
+	/*
+	 * A panel mounted turned in the shell advertises the turn on its connector (a handheld's device
+	 * tree rotation, a monitor's EDID). wlroots reads it but leaves the choice to the compositor;
+	 * without this, everything on such a panel -- the application and the cursor -- is composed the
+	 * wrong way up. Sway does the same when it configures an output.
+	 */
+	if (wlr_output_is_drm(wlr_output)) {
+		enum wl_output_transform transform =
+			wlr_drm_connector_get_panel_orientation(wlr_output);
+		if (transform != WL_OUTPUT_TRANSFORM_NORMAL) {
+			wlr_output_state_set_transform(&state, transform);
+		}
+	}
+#endif
+
 	wlr_log(WLR_DEBUG, "Enabling new output %s", wlr_output->name);
 	if (wlr_output_commit_state(wlr_output, &state)) {
 		output_layout_add_auto(output);
